Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ability to secure policy deployment in dynamic, large-scale environments, ensuring trust, immutability, and rapid rollback.

Key Concepts

- Policy as code and cryptographic signing
- Multi-person approval workflows
- Immutable policy stores and versioning
- SBOM verification for policy modules
- Runtime drift detection and automated rollback
- CI/CD integration and testing strategy

Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch: verify policy signature before loading
function loadPolicy(policy, signature, publicKey) {
  if (!verifySignature(policy, signature, publicKey)) {
    throw new Error("Invalid policy signature");
  }
  applyPolicy(policy);
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate a chain-of-trust failure in CI to catch unsigned changes?
- What are the trade-offs between using an append-only ledger vs a git-based policy store for rollback and auditability?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical security fundamentals at junior level with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Rate limiting and lockout; constant-time comparisons; password hashing; MFA basics; secure logging/privacy.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo login flow illustrating bcrypt compare, MFA, and session issuance
async function login(req, res) {
  const { username, password, mfaToken } = req.body;
  const user = await getUser(username);
  if (!user) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.hash);
  if (!ok) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  if (user.mfaEnabled) {
    if (!mfaToken) return res.status(401).send('MFA required');
    const verified = speakeasy.totp.verify({ secret: user.mfaSecret, encoding: 'base32', token: mfaToken });
    if (!verified) return res.status(401).send('Invalid MFA');
  }
  const session = createSession(user.id);
  res.cookie('session', session, { httpOnly: true, secure: true });
  return res.send({ ok: true });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for distributed rate limiting?
- How would you test lockout and recovery flows?
- How would you store and rotate MFA seeds securely?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical security scenario for chat apps, focusing on end-to-end workflow reliability and implementable controls rather than abstract concepts.

## Key Concepts

- File validation and MIME sniffing
- Malware scanning and sandboxed renderers
- Metadata sanitization and exfiltration risk reduction
- Access control, rate limits, audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic attachment validation skeleton
function validateAttachment(file) {
  if (file.size > MAX_SIZE) return false;
  const mime = detectMime(file); // by magic bytes
  if (!ALLOWED_MIMES.includes(mime)) return false;
  // additional checks: zip slip, nested archives, etc.
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the workflow (unit/integration tests, fuzzing, security scanning reports)?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect abuse or bypass attempts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical skills for rotating credentials without service disruption in a real gateway setup used by large-scale apps.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant API keys with versioning (v1/v2)
- Zero-downtime rollout via grace period
- JWKS-based key distribution and rotation
- Revocation lists and usage auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// pseudo verify function
function verifyToken(token, jwks) {
  const kid = decodeKid(token);
  const key = jwks[kid];
  if (!key) return false;
  return verifySignature(token, key);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the rotation process in staging?
- What metrics would you monitor during the rollout?
- How would you handle rollbacks if issues arise?

## Why This Is Asked
Illustrates mismatch between security controls and high-volume logging. Tests ability to design tamper-evident systems with concrete tech, key management, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Tamper-evident logging
- Signature schemes and KMS/HSM
- Immutable storage
- Key rotation and revocation
- Ingestion verification and alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example signing function using Node.js crypto and AWS KMS
const crypto = require('crypto');
async function signLogEntry(keyId, payload) {
  const signer = crypto.createSign('SHA256');
  signer.update(payload);
  signer.end();
  // In real use, sign with KMS: return await kms.sign({KeyId: keyId, Message: signer.sign()...})
  return signer.sign(null, 'base64');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect a stale signature after key rotation?
- What are the trade-offs of per-event signing vs per-batch signing?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical security hygiene for a common feature: file uploads. It demonstrates understanding of server-side validation, storage separation, malware scanning, and access control patterns.

## Key Concepts

- Server-side validation of size, extensions, and MIME types
- Safe storage with obfuscated filenames
- Malware scanning integration
- Time-limited access URLs

## Code Example

```javascript
const storage = multer.memoryStorage();
const upload = multer({
  limits: { fileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024 },
  fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => {
    const allowed = ['image/png', 'image/jpeg', 'application/pdf'];
    if (allowed.includes(file.mimetype)) cb(null, true);
    else cb(new Error('Invalid file type'), false);
  }
});

app.post('/upload', authenticate, upload.single('file'), async (req, res) => {
  const safeName = `${Date.now()}-${crypto.randomUUID()}${path.extname(req.file.originalname)}`;

  const stream = Readable.from(req.file.buffer);
  await bucket.upload(stream, { destination: safeName });

  await scanFile(bucketName, safeName);

  const [url] = await bucket.getSignedUrl({ action: 'read', expires: Date.now() + 3600000 });
  res.json({ url });
});
```
